  "id": "ch1-classical",
  "title": "The classical: ...e6 and ...c5",
  "intro": "This is the setup you will meet most often. Black plays ...e6 and ...c5, hits the base of White's centre, and develops behind a solid wall. Nothing is threatened, nothing is sharp, and the game is decided by who understands the structure better.\n\n## What Black is doing\n\nThe pawns on d5 and e6 hold the centre and take e4 and c4 away for a while. The cost is the bishop on c8, which is shut in behind them. Black usually solves that with ...b6 and ...Bb7, and those two moves take time and take a defender away from the kingside. That is the trade the whole chapter turns on.\n\n## Your setup, in order\n\nb3 and Bb2, castle, knight to d2, then Ne5. The order barely matters as long as the bishop reaches b2 BEFORE the knight goes to e5, because the bishop is what holds the knight there. Once the knight stands on e5 with the bishop behind it, f4 makes the outpost permanent and opens the f-file, and the rook lifts to f3 and swings to h3.\n\n## The fork you must not miss\n\nOnce White has a knight on e5 and Black has a bishop on d6 and a knight on f6, taking on e5 loses material. After ...Nxe5 the recapture dxe5 attacks BOTH the bishop on d6 and the knight on f6, and Black cannot save them both. If f4 has already been played, fxe5 does the same thing. Count that fork before you allow the trade, and take it when Black allows it.\n\n## When to change plan\n\nIf Black gets the queen to c7 with the bishop on d6, the trade on e5 stops working for you. Then do not play Ne5 at all: take on c5 instead, play c4, and use the bishop on b2 down a diagonal that has just been opened.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nPlaying Ne5 before Bb2. Recapturing on e5 with a piece when the pawn forks two of them. Playing Ne5 into ...Qc7 and ...Bd6 and letting the trade happen on Black's terms. And recapturing on e4 with the bishop when the knight is the piece that should take.",
